GETSOCKOPT(2)                  P32W Unix                   GETSOCKOPT(2)

NAME
       getsockopt, setsockopt, getsockname, getpeername - socket options

SYNOPSIS
       #include <sys/socket.h>
       #include <netinet/in.h>

       int setsockopt(int fd, int level, int name, const void *val,
                      socklen_t len);
       int getsockopt(int fd, int level, int name, void *val,
                      socklen_t *len);
       int getsockname(int fd, struct sockaddr *addr, socklen_t *len);
       int getpeername(int fd, struct sockaddr *addr, socklen_t *len);

DESCRIPTION
       setsockopt with level IPPROTO_TLS and name TLS_HOSTNAME
       gives a TLS socket the server's name (up to 127 bytes, val
       not NUL-terminated, len its length), before connect(2): the
       coprocessor sends it in the handshake and checks the
       certificate against it. SO_REUSEADDR and SO_KEEPALIVE at
       SOL_SOCKET, and options at IPPROTO_TCP, are accepted and do
       nothing: the coprocessor decides.

       getsockopt at SOL_SOCKET: SO_ERROR gives a failed
       connection's errno (0 if none), as after a connect with
       O_NONBLOCK; SO_TYPE gives SOCK_STREAM or SOCK_DGRAM.

       getsockname gives the socket's own address and port,
       getpeername the other end's. *len is the size of addr going
       in, and the address's coming out.

RETURN VALUE
       0; -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       ENOPROTOOPT  An option that is not supported, or TLS_HOSTNAME
                    on a socket that is not TLS.
       ENOTCONN     getpeername: not connected.
       ENOTSOCK     fd is not a socket.

SEE ALSO
       socket(2)
